Derek Ray wrote:

> On 2008-03-14, Janis Papanagnou <Janis_Papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:

>> I think with the "newbie" assumption the damage design WRT fire
>> elementals is insignificant since you usually won't run into such
>> a beast as a newbie.

[snippage]

> I don't know how to explain it any better.  "fire resistance" is
> embodied in many, MANY fantasy games.  In the ones where the concept of
> "complete fire resistance" exists, that resistance is consistent against
> all things.  In others, fire resistance is never more than half damage
> from fire.  The expectation has been set for players across the course
> of over 20 years, in multiple genres and games.

Sorry for hijacking the group once again: in Crawl, some resistance are not
binary (fire and cold need three sources of resistance to be complete;
poison and shock are binary.) But the main point is: there are fire attacks
that go through triple fire resistance (called hellfire), and there are
particularly strong poison attacks getting through poison resistance.
Lightning does just less damage with shock resistance. I have never heard
complains about these concepts (I do hear complains about too high damage
all the time :)

So I think Janis is right. I do not know, how strongly Nethack enforces
the "resistance == foolproof protection" stance. But I'd think way more
than Crawl. (In general, if forced to choose, Nethack would probably go for
the reliable option, and Crawl take the more random one.)

